WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:08.000
When we think of a medieval siege, we picture&nbsp;
knights and siege engines trying to bring a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.840
castle to its knees. But what about the people&nbsp;
who weren’t directly involved in the fighting—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.400
peasants living on the castle’s lands? Were&nbsp;
they attacked too, or were they left alone?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.240
In this video, we’ll explore the fates of farmers,&nbsp;
merchants, and day laborers to see what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:34.000
to them when their lord’s castle came under siege.
Titlescreen&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:41.040
In the spring of 1453, Sultan Mehmed II&nbsp;
attacked Constantinople. The first sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.640
of the Ottoman advance was the forces of the&nbsp;
governor of Rumelia, who marched along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:53.200
coast toward the Golden Horn as an advance guard.&nbsp;
They raided Byzantine villages, burned farms, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.160
tore up vineyards, drawing a belt of destruction&nbsp;
around the city even before the siege had begun.&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.520
This tactic wasn’t unique to the Ottomans—raids&nbsp;
like these were a common first step in attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:12.000
on fortified towns. As historian Clifford Rogers&nbsp;
explains, “farms and properties outside the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.200
being put to the torch ... was a usual opening&nbsp;
move by besieging armies when they arrived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.920
front of a town.” The soldiers were anything but&nbsp;
squeamish during these raids. Entire areas were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.880
devastated, including unfortified villages and&nbsp;
farms. Rogers describes this procedure almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.680
poetically: “Medieval armies normally passed&nbsp;
through hostile territory like a comet through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.360
the heavens, with a solid mass of baggage&nbsp;
and troops in formation, a loose perimeter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.880
of outriders miles out from the center, and&nbsp;
in between a large cloud of men dispersed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:56.000
to conduct the heavily overlapping tasks of&nbsp;
burning, plundering, ravaging, and foraging.”&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.800
These tactics hit rural populations especially&nbsp;
hard—after all, it was their farms, fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.960
and supplies that were plundered, destroyed,&nbsp;
and looted. So, in short, yes—peasants living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:15.200
near a besieged fortress were frequently, if&nbsp;
not always, the subject of targeted attacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:22.000
This was not merely tolerated; it was considered&nbsp;
strategically useful and entirely legitimate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.560
Civilians who supported the defenders by&nbsp;
providing vital supplies were considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.800
valid military targets. From a logistical and&nbsp;
economic standpoint, these raids served to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.200
off enemy resources and apply pressure. But what&nbsp;
did these attacks actually look like? And how did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:47.440
they affect the people who lived through them?
In the Middle Ages, plundering was so commonplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.920
that contemporary historians often&nbsp;
don’t even mention it. When they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.040
they usually resort to vague phrases like:&nbsp;
“The general invaded the enemy’s country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:03.280
and his troops wreaked terrible havoc.” Therefore,&nbsp;
it’s often difficult to know what these invasions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.200
actually looked like in practice. A more vivid&nbsp;
account appears in a contemporary report on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:17.520
revolt of 1173–1174, during which members of Henry&nbsp;
II of England’s own family conspired against him:&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.520
“It was early morning and the dawn scarcely&nbsp;
breaking when the proud company of knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.920
arm themselves. First they attacked the town of&nbsp;
Belford. They spread themselves out over all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:36.320
countryside. Some speed into the farms to wreak&nbsp;
havoc, some take sheep from their folds, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:43.840
some set fire to farmsteads. What more can I tell&nbsp;
you? Never will a tale of greater loss be told.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.520
You could have seen Flemings tying up peasants&nbsp;
and leading them off roped together like heathens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.360
Women flee to the church only to be snatched&nbsp;
away naked, leaving behind their garments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.680
their valuables. ... They burned the countryside;&nbsp;
but God showed his love for these goodly peasants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:08.240
who had no protection, in that their mortal&nbsp;
enemies, the Scots were not there, for they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:15.920
have beaten and killed and ill-treated them.”
This excerpt paints a striking picture of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:22.480
brutality of such raids. In a matter of hours,&nbsp;
the invading men-at-arms devastated the area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.400
around Belford—setting homes ablaze, looting&nbsp;
property, driving off livestock, abducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:36.080
peasants, and assaulting women. The livelihoods of&nbsp;
ordinary people were wiped out almost instantly.&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:43.840
These raids were typically carried out by light&nbsp;
cavalry, infantry, or even non-combatants. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.040
armies even had specialized units for this&nbsp;
purpose—like the Akinji of the Ottoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.840
Empire. In German sources, they are fittingly&nbsp;
referred to as “Renner und Brenner”—literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:02.080
“runners and burners.” This irregular&nbsp;
cavalry received no regular pay;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.000
instead, they made their living entirely&nbsp;
through looting, raiding, and kidnapping.&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.600
Before we look at two of the few surviving&nbsp;
reports on individual experiences, it’s worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.920
exploring the motivation behind these raids,&nbsp;
as well as the purpose they served. Tactically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.240
such raids fulfilled several key functions. By&nbsp;
devastating—that is, literally emptying out or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:32.400
laying waste to—the surrounding area, armies&nbsp;
achieved three main goals: soldiers enriched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.360
themselves through plundering, the army&nbsp;
gathered supplies and food through foraging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:44.400
and the enemy suffered serious economic damage.
During a siege, sudden raids also served to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.720
catch defenders off guard, preventing them from&nbsp;
stockpiling supplies or reinforcing the fortress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.680
This caused the stronghold to run out of&nbsp;
food and resources more quickly—especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.240
when civilians from the surrounding&nbsp;
countryside fled to the castle for safety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.800
overwhelming already limited space and provisions.
Looting and pillaging also put enormous pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:13.120
on the territorial lord. He was responsible for&nbsp;
protecting his subjects—if he allowed the enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:19.440
to ravage his lands without resistance, he was&nbsp;
seen as failing in his duty. In fact, the extent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.440
of devastation was often interpreted as a sign&nbsp;
of one side’s superiority and, by extension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.760
its victory. Those who carried out the plundering&nbsp;
were considered the winners; those who endured it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:37.600
the losers. The greater the destruction,&nbsp;
the more decisive the triumph. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:44.560
no prince could accept such a situation without a&nbsp;
fight. As a result, plundering became a practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.320
tool to pressure a sovereign—sometimes even&nbsp;
forcing him to leave the safety of his castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.880
and engage in battle. So, raiding peasants was&nbsp;
more than a random act of violence; it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:03.360
strategic lever within a larger military plan.
Overall, plundering was one of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.520
tools in medieval warfare. Cavalry raids or&nbsp;
chevauchées—in which mounted units like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:16.000
Akinji ravaged enemy territory for weeks—were a&nbsp;
common tactic across Europe and left a deep mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.520
on the war experience of ordinary people. Henry V&nbsp;
of England is famously reported to have said, “War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:30.400
without fire is as worthless as sausages without&nbsp;
mustard.” .’ Henry is, of course, the English king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.920
who led his men to that incredible victory at the&nbsp;
Battle of Agincourt in 1415 during the Hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.960
Years' War. If you want to dive deeper into the&nbsp;
Hundred Years War, we can't recommend Bernard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.480
Cornwell's The Grail Quest series enough. This&nbsp;
is a genuine shoutout by the way; not a sponsored&nbsp;&nbsp;

