The reason was simple: damaging the enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.400
through plundering was often easier and far less&nbsp;
risky than facing them in battle or siege. Even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.520
small group of men with little combat experience&nbsp;
could cause significant destruction this way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.120
These attacks rarely targeted fortified towns;&nbsp;
instead, they focused on vulnerable villages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:00.880
settlements, and farms—striking a rural&nbsp;
population that was usually weakly defended.&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:06.080
The fate of individual people often depended on&nbsp;
the circumstances and intentions of the invading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:11.040
army. If the attackers were in a hurry, they&nbsp;
focused on seizing what they needed by clearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.560
out warehouses, cellars, and barns. If their goal&nbsp;
was to inflict the most damage in the shortest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.160
amount of time, their most effective weapon&nbsp;
was fire. When an army set fire to thatched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:29.040
roofs and ripe grain fields—as in the revolt of&nbsp;
1173–1174—it could destroy entire swaths of land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.880
as it passed through. For the peasants caught in&nbsp;
these raids, the consequences were devastating:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:41.120
within hours, they could lose both their&nbsp;
livelihoods and the roofs over their heads.&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:47.440
When an army had time—or even stayed in one place,&nbsp;
as during a siege—the plundering often became even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:54.080
worse. Simply put, the slower an army moved, the&nbsp;
more thoroughly it looted and the greater the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:00.960
economic damage it inflicted. Sieges took this&nbsp;
to the extreme, since attackers had to resupply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.720
over long periods deep in enemy territory.&nbsp;
To gather enough food, they squeezed every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:12.640
last chicken and bushel of grain from the local&nbsp;
population. Soldiers hunted for hidden camps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:19.440
interrogated prisoners, scoured forests and ponds,&nbsp;
rounded up scattered livestock, and more—in short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:26.480
they turned the enemy’s lands upside down, leaving&nbsp;
them with nothing but the bitter sting of famine.&nbsp;

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:32.320
When an army aimed to cause maximum damage, it&nbsp;
sometimes resorted to more elaborate tactics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.720
For example, if the grain wasn’t ripe enough to&nbsp;
burn, they would trample it. They also destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:45.280
irrigation systems or broke through dams. Even&nbsp;
hard, backbreaking labor was undertaken. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:51.200
example, clearing vineyards and olive groves—like&nbsp;
the Ottomans did near Constantinople—often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:57.680
required cutting down trees for days on end. This&nbsp;
made the long-term damage even more devastating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.800
For example, during the crusade against the&nbsp;
Albigenses—a religious community in southern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.400
France declared heretics by the Pope—the crusaders&nbsp;
destroyed the vineyards around Toulouse during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:14.800
siege. This was especially brutal for the locals,&nbsp;
since it takes years for vines to produce a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:22.000
harvest. They had to watch in despair as the&nbsp;
attackers wiped out the work of generations.&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:27.360
Beyond the methods and scale of looting,&nbsp;
much remains unknown—largely because medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:33.440
historians showed little interest in the fate of&nbsp;
peasants. One rare exception is Martin Kotbus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.200
a merchant from the village of Bolkenhain (now&nbsp;
Bolków, Poland), who left behind some vivid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.120
accounts of the Hussite Wars. Everything we know&nbsp;
about Martin himself comes from a report he wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:49.840
in 1444, the year a band of Bohemian warriors&nbsp;
attacked his hometown. Martin describes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:55.440
brutal raid in striking detail. The men plundered&nbsp;
the streets, dragged families from their homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:01.520
and systematically searched for valuables. They&nbsp;
eventually reached Martin’s house. Armed guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.000
took up positions in the street, allowing&nbsp;
their comrades to ransack the residents’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.920
belongings undisturbed and carry off household&nbsp;
goods. They turned everything upside down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:17.760
taking what they wanted and destroying what they&nbsp;
didn’t. The nightmare only ended when they forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:23.120
their way into the room of Martin’s wife, who had&nbsp;
recently given birth and was still bedridden—not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:28.960
out of mercy, but because some of the attackers&nbsp;
knew her personally. They then stopped and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:36.000
withdrew, even warning the family to hide in the&nbsp;
cellar, as they planned to burn down the town.&nbsp;

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:41.760
As in Martin’s case, the fate of peasants&nbsp;
often came down to chance—and the whims of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:48.400
their tormentors. According to Malte Prietzel, an&nbsp;
expert on medieval warfare, those hoping for mercy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:54.720
were almost always disappointed. While medieval&nbsp;
texts often speak of a knightly duty to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:00.880
the weak, the poor, and women, the historical&nbsp;
record tells a different story. In practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:06.560
nearly all soldiers engaged in plundering when&nbsp;
operating in enemy territory—even Christian armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:12.480
attacking fellow Christian communities. Often,&nbsp;
they didn’t even spare churches or monasteries.&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.440
Attacks were especially brutal during&nbsp;
wars against people of different cultures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:23.360
other faiths, or those labeled heretics, who were&nbsp;
seen as traitors to the faith and could expect no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:30.400
mercy. One striking example comes from a report on&nbsp;
the Lithuanian Crusade. In it, the poet and herald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:37.360
Peter Suchenwirt describes how a group of knights&nbsp;
raided a Lithuanian wedding: “There was a wedding:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:44.400
the guests came uninvited! They made a dance&nbsp;
with the heathens, so that probably 60 of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:51.440
lay lifeless. Then they set fire to the village&nbsp;
so that the flames flickered high into the air. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:57.440
swear I wouldn’t have wanted to be the bridegroom&nbsp;
at that wedding—the bride would have chased me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:04.560
away with ease.” Perhaps most shocking is not&nbsp;
Suchenwirt’s description of the massacre itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:10.800
but the casual tone with which he recounts&nbsp;
it. Under his pen, the brutal attack becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:17.280
playful anecdote, the slaughter of defenseless&nbsp;
villagers framed as a bold and even humorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:25.520
act. There’s no trace of remorse—only pride.
The peasants living in the area around a besieged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:32.160
castle weren’t just occasionally affected—they&nbsp;
were often a primary target of the attackers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:37.120
Looting was a core element of medieval warfare,&nbsp;
and the unprotected rural population made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:43.120
for ideal victims. Exactly how these raids&nbsp;
unfolded, and what happened to the peasants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:48.800
is only known in a handful of individual cases.&nbsp;
More often than not, their fate was determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:54.800
by chance and the whims of their attackers.&nbsp;
If they were lucky, they “only” lost some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.800 --> 00:15:02.880
valuables. If they weren’t, they lost their&nbsp;
livelihood, their freedom, or their lives.&nbsp;

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.760
Thanks for watching. Researching and bringing&nbsp;
you these lesser-known stories from history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:14.000
takes time and care, and we rely on your support&nbsp;
to keep doing it. For just $3 a month on Patreon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:19.760
you can help us dig deeper into topics like this —&nbsp;
